  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Sardinia has seven thousand stone towers and no written explanation. They are called nuraghi. Cones of stacked dark stone, the tallest about twenty metres.",
        "visual_query": "sardinia stone tower ruins landscape",
        "visual_fallback": "stone ruins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "They went up in the Bronze Age, from roughly the eighteenth century BC onward. Individual blocks run to several tons each.",
        "visual_query": "bronze age megalithic stone blocks",
        "visual_fallback": "megalithic stones"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Nothing bonds them. The walls hold on the weight of their own stones, leaning inward until they close into a dome overhead. And nobody knows what they were for.",
        "visual_query": "corbelled stone dome chamber interior",
        "visual_fallback": "stone chamber"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Fortresses. Chiefs' houses. Temples. Meeting halls. Archaeologists have argued every one of those, and there is still no agreement.",
        "visual_query": "sardinian hilltop ruins excavation site",
        "visual_fallback": "hilltop ruins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then a 2002 survey measured the doorways. A great many of them face the winter solstice sunrise, which means whatever else these towers were, somebody was lining them up with the sky.",
        "visual_query": "winter solstice sunrise over stones",
        "visual_fallback": "sunrise stones"
      }
    ],
